language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ devils_word: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ Given an array of integers, insert '+' or '-' operators before each integer in the array so that summing them up will result in a given total (666 for the devil's word).
$
$ Save this file as model.eprime. Download `devils_word.json` and `dcp_convert_essence.py` from the
$ problem's page:
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/devils_word.html
$ Convert the instances once, then run the first one with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the
$ solution is written to `devils_word_1.param.solution`:
$   python dcp_convert_essence.py devils_word.json
$   savilerow model.eprime devils_word_1.param -run-solver

$ arr_dim1: the number of integers (derived from the data; not a field of the reader's JSON)
given arr_dim1 : int
letting n = arr_dim1
$ arr[i]: the i-th integer of the array, indices 1-based (the data's index 0 is arr[1])
given arr : matrix indexed by [int(1..n)] of int
$ the total that the signed sum must reach
given total : int

$ plus[i]: true when the i-th integer gets a '+' sign, false when it gets a '-' sign
find plus : matrix indexed by [int(1..n)] of bool
$ result[i]: the i-th integer with its sign applied (the output, indices 1-based)
$ a value is +-arr[i], so it is at most the sum of the absolute values of arr in size
letting bound = sum i : int(1..n) . |arr[i]|
find result : matrix indexed by [int(1..n)] of int(-bound..bound)

branching on [plus]
such that
$ each integer is signed: with a '+' it keeps its value, with a '-' it is negated
forAll i : int(1..n) . (plus[i] -> result[i] = arr[i]) /\ (!plus[i] -> result[i] = -arr[i]),
$ the signed integers sum to the given total
(sum i : int(1..n) . result[i]) = total
